const euler2Cfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: 'e^x = 1 + x + x²/2! + x³/3! + …', note: 'The exponential\'s power series — valid for every x. The bold move of the 1700s: plug in what isn\'t real and see if the pattern still speaks.' },
    { math: 'e^{iθ} = 1 + iθ + (iθ)²/2! + …', note: 'Substitute x = iθ. Powers of i cycle: i, −1, −i, 1, i, −1…' },
    { math: '= (1 − θ²/2! + θ⁴/4! − …) + i(θ − θ³/3! + …)', note: 'Sort real and imaginary terms — and recognise the two famous series hiding inside.' },
    { math: 'e^{iθ} = cos θ + i·sin θ', note: 'Euler\'s formula. Rotation, hidden inside the exponential — which is why engineers write waves as e^{iωt}.' },
    { math: 'θ = π: e^{iπ} + 1 = 0', note: 'Five fundamental constants, one line. When mathematicians vote for the most beautiful equation, this wins.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/expert/complex-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Complex analysis is calculus on functions whose inputs and outputs live in the plane of
    a + bi. Its miracle: demanding that a function be differentiable in the <em>complex</em>
    sense is so restrictive that everything becomes rigid and beautiful. Complex-differentiable
    functions are infinitely smooth, determined by their values on any tiny patch, and obey
    integral formulas that feel like magic until the rigidity sinks in. And the whole subject
    is secretly trigonometry grown up: the unit circle, the exact values, the sine and cosine
    of the school syllabus are the local shadows of one complex function, e to the iθ. Every
    section below takes a trigonometry topic and shows the complex machinery underneath it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Euler's formula, derived honestly — the series substitution that married exponentials to rotation" widget="equationStep" config={euler2Cfg} />

  <h2>Pythagoras and the unit circle: where complex numbers live</h2>
  <p>
    Pythagoras' theorem (E6.1) is the metric of the plane: a distance is √(x² + y²). Complex
    numbers adopt it as their definition of size — the modulus |z| = √(a² + b²) — and the
    unit circle |z| = 1 becomes the home of all of trigonometry. The theorem's algebraic face
    is older and stranger than the geometric one: a Pythagorean triple (3, 4, 5) is a rational
    point on the unit circle, and every triple comes from the parametrisation (1 − t², 2t,
    1 + t²): take t = ½ and you get (3/4, 1, 5/4), which scales by 4 to the 3-4-5. The same circle, read over the
    complex numbers, is where rotation lives: multiplying by a unit-complex number is exactly
    a rotation, since moduli multiply (1 × 1 = 1) and arguments add. Even Fermat's Last
    Theorem — no whole-number solutions to xⁿ + yⁿ = zⁿ for n above 2 — is the statement that
    the Pythagorean circle has no higher-dimensional cousins, a claim proved only in 1995
    (Wiles) by methods that run straight through complex analysis.
  </p>

  <h2>Right-angled triangles: SOH-CAH-TOA becomes polar coordinates</h2>
  <p>
    Right-triangle trigonometry (E6.2) defines sine and cosine as ratios — opposite over
    hypotenuse — valid only inside the triangle. The unit circle liberates them: sine and
    cosine become coordinates of a point at angle θ, defined for every real θ, negative
    angles included. Complex analysis completes the liberation: any complex number is
    z = r(cos θ + i sin θ), its <strong>polar form</strong>, which Euler's formula compresses
    to z = re^(iθ). Multiplication becomes the star rule: <em>multiply moduli, add angles</em>.
    Two quick checks: (1 + i) × (1 − i) has moduli √2 each and arguments +45° and −45°, so
    the product has modulus 2 and argument 0 — it equals 2, exactly as the algebra says.
    Square (1 + i) and you get modulus 2, angle 90° — which is 2i, again exact. The school
    rules for surds and the geometry of rotating arrows are one subject; the polar form is
    the dictionary, and it is why engineers write every oscillation as a rotating complex
    exponential rather than a pair of sines.
  </p>

  <h2>Exact values: roots of unity and the golden ratio</h2>
  <p>
    Exact trigonometric values (E6.3) — sin 30° = ½, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 36° = (1 + √5)/4 —
    look like isolated trophies. They are solutions of polynomial equations, and complex
    analysis explains which angles get exact forms. The equation zⁿ = 1 has exactly n complex
    solutions, the <strong>roots of unity</strong>, evenly spaced around the unit circle at
    angles 360°/n; their real parts are exact cosines, and solving the polynomial (for n = 5,
    a quartic) produces the golden ratio inside cos 36° — the pentagram's secret, algebraised.
    The pattern even predicts its own limits: which regular polygons can be constructed with
    ruler and compasses is exactly the question "when is the n-th cyclotomic polynomial
    solvable by nested square roots?", and Gauss answered it at 19 (1796), discovering the
    constructibility of the 17-gon. The exact-value table is the visible tip of a theory about
    when radicals can reach rotation — and complex numbers are the reach.
  </p>

  <h2>Trigonometric functions: one exponential, many waves</h2>
  <p>
    The trigonometric functions (E6.4) — graphs, periods, identities — condense to a single
    line: e^(iθ) = cos θ + i sin θ. Every identity becomes bookkeeping. Add angles: multiply
    exponentials. The Pythagorean identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 is |e^(iθ)|² = 1. De Moivre's
    theorem (cos θ + i sin θ)ⁿ = cos nθ + i sin nθ is literally the power rule (e^(iθ))ⁿ =
    e^(inθ). And the expert payoff is Fourier analysis: any reasonable signal — a violin's
    sound, a JPEG's pixels, a radio broadcast — decomposes into a sum of pure exponentials,
    because they are the eigenfunctions of time-translation: shift a pure tone and you merely
    multiply it by a constant. MP3 and JPEG compression, radio tuning and the solving of the
    heat and wave equations are all the statement "complex
    exponentials are the natural alphabet of oscillation", which is Euler's formula writ
    large. The trig functions stop being ratios in triangles and become the coordinate
    system of frequency.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quantum mechanics is the deepest customer. States are vectors with complex
    components, observables are operators, and time evolution is multiplication by
    e^(−iEt/ħ) — the same exponential, with energy inside the argument. Interference,
    the subject's heart, is complex addition: two paths to the same outcome add their
    amplitudes, and the relative phase (an angle, exactly as in polar form) decides
    whether they reinforce or cancel — the double-slit experiment's pattern is
    literally the modulus-squared of a complex sum. Schrödinger chose complex
    amplitudes not for elegance but because they are the minimal algebra in which
    oscillation and rotation can be expressed; complex analysis is the calculus of
    that choice, and the path-integral formulation of quantum field theory — summing
    e^(iS/ħ) over histories — runs on the same one-liner.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "sin and cos are about triangles." By the end of this page
    they are characters in the complex exponential — defined for complex arguments too
    (sin z grows exponentially in the imaginary direction, which surprises everyone the first
    time). The triangle definitions are the special case θ real; treating them as the
    definition is what makes identities hard and Fourier theory impossible.
  </p>

  <h2>Non-right-angled triangles: the oblique geometry of the plane</h2>
  <p>
    Non-right triangles (E6.5) need the two oblique rules. The cosine rule, c² = a² + b² −
    2ab cos C, is Pythagoras with a correction term that vanishes exactly when C = 90° —
    the school theorem as a special case, not a separate fact. Worked: a = 5, b = 7, C = 60°
    gives c² = 25 + 49 − 70 × 0.5 = 39, so c = √39 ≈ 6.24. The sine rule, a/sin A = b/sin B,
    hides a deeper constant: every ratio equals 2R, the <em>diameter of the circumcircle</em> —
    the triangle is always inscribed in exactly one circle, and the sine rule is its
    equation. Complex analysis generalises both at a stroke: the complex plane's distance is
    Pythagoras, and the functions that respect it are the <strong>conformal maps</strong> —
    analytic functions preserve angles locally, bending triangles into other triangles with
    angles preserved (sides not). That is the subject's engineering face: flow around an
    aerofoil is solved by conformally mapping the aerofoil to a circle where the flow is
    easy (Joukowski, 1910), and cartographers map the sphere to the plane the same way —
    Mercator's projection is conformal, which is why compass bearings are straight lines on
    it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "complex numbers are a bookkeeping trick with no
    geometric meaning; the 'real' mathematics is the real numbers." The plane of complex
    numbers is arguably <em>more</em> geometric than the real line: rotation — which the real
    line cannot even express — is multiplication there. Trigonometry, waves and rotations
    become algebra; the "imaginary" axis is the direction of a quarter-turn, and the name is
    a historical apology, not a description.
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "e^(iπ) + 1 = 0 is a numerical coincidence — a cute
    pattern in the digits." It is a theorem with a mechanism: the exponential is defined by
    its power series, the series evaluated at iπ splits into the cosine series at π and the
    sine series at π, giving −1 + 0. The five constants meet because rotation (π) and growth
    (e) are two views of one function — the widget above runs the argument line by line.
    Calling it a coincidence is like calling the Pythagorean theorem a coincidence of the
    3-4-5 triangle.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Rigidity, quantified.</strong> Theorems that shock real-variable intuition:
    if a function is complex-differentiable once on a region, it is automatically infinitely
    differentiable there and equals its Taylor series (real calculus has smooth
    non-analytic functions, like e^(−1/x²); complex calculus has none). Liouville's theorem:
    a bounded function differentiable on the whole plane must be constant — so e^(iz) must
    blow up somewhere, and it does, along the imaginary axis. The identity theorem: zeros
    cannot hide — accumulate at any point and the function is identically zero, which is why
    one table of values pins down a whole analytic function. The maximum modulus principle:
    |f| cannot peak in the interior unless f is constant. Each is a quantitative version of
    one idea — complex differentiability is impossibly strong a condition — and together they
    make analytic functions the most predictable objects in mathematics.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Residues: integration as contouring.</strong> Cauchy's theorem says the integral
    of an analytic function around any closed loop is zero — so integrals detect only the
    <em>holes</em>, the points where a function blows up. Each such point carries a residue
    (the coefficient of 1/(z − z₀) in its expansion), and the residue theorem computes a loop
    integral as 2πi times the enclosed residues. This is not exotic: the Fourier transform of
    a decaying signal, the integrals behind dispersion relations in particle physics, and the
    classic ∫ (from minus infinity to infinity) of 1/(1 + x²) dx = π all fall to a one-line
    residue count. Real integrals that resist every real-variable technique surrender to
    contour deformation in the complex plane — the standard working method of theoretical
    physics.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The Riemann hypothesis.</strong> The subject's deepest open problem lives here.
    The Riemann zeta function ζ(s) = 1 + 1/2ˢ + 1/3ˢ + … extends (analytically — this is
    where that word earns its keep) to the whole complex plane except s = 1, and its zeros
    organise the primes: Riemann (1859) showed the prime-counting function is governed by
    the zeros' positions. The hypothesis — all the non-trivial zeros lie exactly on the line
    Re(s) = 1/2 — would give the sharpest possible error bound on how evenly primes are
    distributed. It is one of the Clay Millennium Problems, verified numerically to
    astronomical heights and proved for nothing close to all cases; its resolution will run
    through the rigidity machinery this page introduced, which is why every serious
    complex-analysis course ends by pointing at it.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Needham, Visual Complex Analysis; Ahlfors, Complex Analysis.</p>
</TopicPage>
